     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "\"```java\\npublic class PrintNumbers {\\n\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        // Using a for loop to iterate from 1 to 10\\n        for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {\\n            System.out.println(i);\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**Explanation:**\\n\\n1. **`public class PrintNumbers { ... }`**: This defines a class named `PrintNumbers`.  In Java, all code must reside within a class.\\n\\n2. **`public static void main(String[] args) { ... }`**: This is the main method, the entry point of the Java program.  Execution begins here.\\n   - `public`:  Makes the method accessible from anywhere.\\n   - `static`: Means the method belongs to the class itself, not an instance of the class.\\n   - `void`: Indicates that the method doesn't return any value.\\n   - `main(String[] args)`: The standard entry point for Java applications.  `String[] args` allows command-line arguments to be passed to the program.\\n\\n3. **`for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) { ... }`**: This is a `for` loop.  It's a fundamental control flow statement in Java.\\n   - `int i = 1;`:  Initializes a variable `i` to 1.  `i` will serve as our loop counter.  We begin with a starting value of 1.\\n   - `i <= 10;`:  This is the loop condition.  The loop will continue to execute as long as `i` is less than or equal to 10.\\n   - `i++`: This is the increment statement. After each iteration of the loop, the value of `i` is incremented by 1.\\n\\n4. **`System.out.println(i);`**: This line inside the loop prints the current value of `i` to the console. `System.out.println()` is a standard Java method used to print output to the console.\\n\\n**How to compile and run the code:**\\n\\n1. **Save the code:** Save the code as `PrintNumbers.java`.\\n2. **Compile:** Open a terminal or command prompt and navigate to the directory where you saved the file. Then compile the code using the Java compiler:\\n   ```bash\\n   javac PrintNumbers.java\\n   ```\\n3. **Run:**  After successful compilation, run the compiled program:\\n   ```bash\\n   java PrintNumbers\\n   ```\\n\\nThis will print the numbers from 1 to 10 to your console, each on a new line.\\n\""
      ]
